# ===========================================================================
# HIVE-LOCAL — .gitignore
# ===========================================================================
# The default in this repository is to COMMIT, not to ignore. CLAUDE.md §1 makes
# that the rule the whole rehearsal rests on: every artifact is a declarative file
# committed to git, because this build is migrated to the official platform later
# and anything that exists only on this laptop does not survive the move.
#
# So this file is deliberately short, and each entry is one of exactly three kinds:
#
#   (1) MACHINE-LOCAL NOISE — editor and OS droppings, nothing to do with HIVE.
#   (2) REGENERABLE OUTPUT — installed dependencies and build products, recreated
#       from a committed lockfile or manifest.
#   (3) CREDENTIALS AND RUNTIME STATE — things that MUST NOT be committed, either
#       because they are secrets or because they are the cluster's truth rather
#       than the repo's intent.
#
# Anything that does not fall into one of those three does not belong here.
# In particular, see the two "DELIBERATELY NOT IGNORED" notes at the bottom.
# ===========================================================================

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# (1) macOS and editors
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
.DS_Store
.AppleDouble
.LSOverride
._*
.Spotlight-V100
.Trashes
*.swp
*~
.idea/
.vscode/*
!.vscode/extensions.json

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# (2) Python — the Studio backend
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The venv is machine-local by construction: it is built against whichever
# interpreter this laptop happens to have, and VERSIONS.md already flags that
# CPython 3.14 may need to become a 3.11/3.13 venv for studio/backend/ if a
# dependency will not build. The DEPENDENCY LIST is the committed artifact;
# the resolved environment is not.
.venv/
venv/
env/
__pycache__/
*.py[cod]
*.egg-info/
.pytest_cache/
.mypy_cache/
.ruff_cache/

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# (2) Node — the Studio frontend
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
node_modules/
dist/
.vite/
*.tsbuildinfo
npm-debug.log*
yarn-error.log*

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# (3) Credentials — kubeconfigs, above all the per-tenant ones
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# `vcluster connect <name> -n p-<name> --print` emits a kubeconfig containing an
# ADMIN CREDENTIAL for that tenant's control plane. The inference-endpoint
# template hands exactly such a file to the PI as its `kubeconfig` output, so the
# Studio backend will be producing these routinely. None of them may ever land in
# a commit.
kubeconfig
kubeconfig.yaml
*.kubeconfig
.kube/

# Generic secret shapes. There is no identity layer locally (DEVIATIONS.md), so
# there is nothing here to leak TODAY — these exist so that the day Keycloak
# arrives, the ignore rule is already in place rather than being remembered.
.env
.env.*
!.env.example
*.pem
*.key

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# (3) Studio runtime state
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The registry. CLAUDE.md Studio invariant (b) splits truth in two: the CLUSTER
# is the source of truth for what IS, and this SQLite file records only what was
# ASKED FOR — who requested what, when, and how the pipeline went. That makes it
# a local operations log, not a declarative artifact: it is written continuously
# at runtime, it is binary, it would conflict on every pull, and a committed copy
# would describe deployments that no longer exist on anyone else's machine.
studio/backend/studio.db
studio/backend/studio.db-journal
studio/backend/studio.db-wal
studio/backend/studio.db-shm

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# (3) Scratch
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
*.log
*.tmp
/tmp/

# The UI checks' scratch build. scripts/studio-ui-check.sh compiles
# studio/frontend/tests/ui-check.jsx here with Vite and runs it in Node; it is
# regenerated every run and holds nothing a person would read.
studio/frontend/.ui-check/

# ===========================================================================
# (n) A PI's OWN DOCUMENTS — staged corpora
# ===========================================================================
# POST /api/uploads streams a PI's uploaded documents to tenants/_uploads/<id>/,
# and the RAG pipeline's corpus-upload step copies from there onto the project's
# `scratch` volume. THEY ARE RESEARCH DATA, not repository content: they belong
# to whoever uploaded them, they may be unpublished, and the wizard's own copy
# promises they are "copied into your project once, at setup, and deleted when
# the project is". Committing them would break that promise in the most durable
# way available.
#
# The leading underscore is why `_uploads` cannot collide with a project
# directory: projectName is constrained to ^[a-z0-9-]{3,20}$ and cannot begin
# with one. So this rule can never accidentally ignore a real tenant.
#
# THE DEMO CORPUS IS DIFFERENT AND IS COMMITTED —
# templates/catalog/rag-stack/demo-corpus/ is part of the catalogue, written for
# this repository, and is what gets indexed when a PI uploads nothing.
tenants/_uploads/

# ===========================================================================
# DELIBERATELY NOT IGNORED — do not "tidy" these away
# ===========================================================================
#
# tenants/*/rendered/
#   Studio invariant (a) — render before apply. Studio writes every manifest to
#   tenants/<project>/rendered/ BEFORE applying it, and applies the file it wrote.
#   Those files are the evidence that a Studio-provisioned project is as
#   reviewable, diffable and reproducible as a hand-written one. They look like
#   build output and they are not: they are the artifact the invariant exists to
#   produce. Ignoring them would silently break the §1 rule with a UI in front of it.
#
# host/serving/**, host/cni/**  (~263,000 lines of vendored upstream YAML)
#   cert-manager, Knative Serving, net-kourier, KServe and Calico are vendored
#   rather than applied from a URL, so the EXACT BYTES installed are in git and a
#   re-run is reproducible offline — which is the demo condition (CLAUDE.md §6).
#   They are marked linguist-vendored in .gitattributes so they do not distort
#   GitHub's language statistics; that is a display concern, not an ignore rule.
# ===========================================================================
